6.text:
With one regressor and one instrument the Nagar first order bias can be
written, up to $o_{p}(1)$, as

\begin{equation*}
 {\rm E}\big[\hat \beta_{2SLS}-\beta\mid Z\big]
      \;\simeq\;\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\frac{k}{\mu^{2}},\qquad
\mu^{2}=\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\Pi/\sigma_{v}^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that with the usual homoscedastic first stage

\begin{equation*}
  r(\mu^{2})\equiv
  \frac{{\rm E}\big[\hat \beta_{2SLS}-\beta\big]}
       {{\rm E}\big[\hat \beta_{OLS}-\beta\big]}
  \;\simeq\;\frac{k}{\mu^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

For $k=1$ $r\simeq\mu^{-2}$ and $r\simeq (E F-1)^{-1}$ in the conventional
notation.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
r\simeq\frac1{F}\qquad{\rm i.e.\qquad}
\frac{{\rm plim}\; {\rm bias}_{2SLS}}
     {{\rm plim}\; {\rm bias}_{OLS}}
\approx\frac1{F}\approx\frac{k}{\mu^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

Hence $r=0.10$ corresponds to the concentration

\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}_{0.10}=\frac{k}{0.10}=10\;,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\mu^{2}/k=10$.  For $n\to\infty$ the first stage

\begin{equation*}
F\;\xrightarrow{d}\;\frac{\chi^{2}_{k}(\mu^{2})}{k}
\end{equation*}

and for $k=1$

\begin{equation*}
F\;\stackrel{a}{\sim}\;(Z+\sqrt{\mu^{2}})^{2},
\qquad Z\sim N(0,1).
\end{equation*}

Let $c_{\alpha}$ be the $1-\alpha$ quantile of $F$ under
$\mu^{2}=\mu^{2}_{0.10}$.  On the $1-\alpha$ one–sided test

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb P}\!\left(F>c_{\alpha}\mid\mu^{2}=\mu^{2}_{0.10}\right)=\alpha,
\end{equation*}

${\mathbb P}\!\left(\mu^{2}\ge\mu^{2}_{0.10}\mid F>c_{\alpha}\right)
\ge1-\alpha$, i.e. an observed $F>c_{\alpha}$ gives $1-\alpha$ confidence
that $r\le0.10$.  With the $1$ d.f. representation

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb P}\!\left((Z+\sqrt{\mu^{2}})^{2}\le c\right)
 =\Phi\!\left(\sqrt c-\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\right)
   -\Phi\!\left(-\sqrt c-\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\right)\approx
 \Phi\!\left(\sqrt c-\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\right).
\end{equation*}

Putting $\alpha=0.05$ and $\mu^{2}=10$, $\sqrt{\mu^{2}}=3.162$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi\!\left(\sqrt c-3.162\right)=0.95\ \Longleftrightarrow\
\sqrt c-3.162=z_{0.95}=1.645,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{c_{0.95}}\approx3.162+1.645=4.81,\qquad
c_{0.95}\approx4.81^{2}\approx 23.1,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $F_{1-\alpha}\approx 23$ for the conventional
$Nagar\;r\simeq k/\mu^{2}$ approximation, $F_{0.95}\approx1+\chi^{2}_{0.95}
(10)\simeq24$ in the $E F=1+\mu^{2}/k$ notation.  This is the usual
Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal–relative–bias critical value,
$c_{0.10}\simeq23.1$, often quoted as $F\gtrsim 23$–$24$; in particular
$F\approx11$ is the point estimate $r\approx1/F\approx0.10$ and $F\approx
23$ is the $95\%$ one–sided confidence counterpart.  With the
conventional $F>10$ rule of thumb this is the $ \approx 23$–$24$ Stock-Yogo
$10\%$ relative bias threshold, essentially $F\gtrsim 23$ in practice,
$\approx24$ if the $+1$ mean correction is kept.

19.text:
Under homoskedasticity the first stage with one instrument can be written

\begin{equation*}
F\simeq \frac{\chi^{2}_{1}(\mu^{2})}{1}\,,\qquad 
\mu^{2}=n\pi^{2}\sigma_{z}^{2}/\sigma_{v}^{2},
\end{equation*}
and, to first Nagar order,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{{\rm Bias}\big(\hat\beta_{2SLS}\big)}
     {{\rm Bias}\big(\hat\beta_{OLS}\big)}
 \;\approx\;-\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}\;\approx\;-\frac1{F}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that in the point estimate $|\,{\rm rel\,bias}\,|\approx F^{-1}$.
For a point estimate $|\,{\rm rel\,bias}\,|<0.10$ we need 
$\mu^{2}>10$ i.e. $F>10$.

Let $Y=L F$.  Up to the usual $n\to\infty$ denominator $Y\stackrel{a}{\sim}
\chi^{2}_{L}(\mu^{2})$.  With $L=1$

\begin{equation*}
Y=(Z+\sqrt{\mu^{2}})^{2}, \qquad Z\sim N(0,1),
\end{equation*}
so that, to the usual Patnaik approximation,

\begin{equation*}
P\!\left(Y\le y\mid\mu^{2}\right)
      =\Phi\!\left(\sqrt y-\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\right)
       -\Phi\!\left(-\sqrt y-\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\right)
      \approx\Phi\!\left(\sqrt y-\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\right).
\end{equation*}
A one sided $95\%$ lower confidence bound for $\mu^{2}$ from an observed
$y_{0}=F_{0}$ is obtained from

\begin{equation*}
P\!\left(Y\le y_{0}\mid\mu^{2}_{L}\right)=0.95,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  $\sqrt{y_{0}}-\sqrt{\mu^{2}_{L}}\approx z_{0.95}=1.645$.
Hence the $95\%$ lower bound is $\mu^{2}_{L}\approx
\bigl(\sqrt{y_{0}}-1.645\bigr)^{2}$.  Requiring $\mu^{2}_{L}\ge 10$
gives

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{0}}\;\approx\;\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F_{0}^{*}\;\approx\;\Big(\sqrt{10}+1.645\Big)^{2}\approx 2.3\!-\!2.4\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
about $F_{0}^{*}\approx 23$–$24$, in practice $\approx 23$ on the
$\chi^{2}/{1}$ approximation, $F_{0}^{*}\approx 22$–$26$ on the usual
non–central $F_{1,\infty}$ inversion.  In particular the familiar
point estimate $F>10$ for $|\,{\rm rel\,bias}\,|<0.10$ is pushed up to
$F\approx 20$–$25$ once the $95\%$ sampling uncertainty in the first
stage $F$ is allowed for, the textbook Stock–Yogo $95\%$ value being
$\approx 23$–$24$, essentially the $F\approx 23$ rule of thumb.  With the
conventional two–sided $1.96$ normal approximation this is $F\approx
23$–$31$, e.g. $F-1.96\sqrt{2F}=10$ gives $F\approx 23.4$, the
$F\approx 10$ point rule blown up by the sampling error in the first
stage.

